  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+5x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(2,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(2,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,2)$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-12x+5 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =6x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{\u2033}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-12 & >0\\,\u27f9\\,x>2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the function is concave up on $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{\u2033}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $6x-12<0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty ,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
